  "headline": "Oil-fed inflation and a flattening curve say tightening, not reflation",
  "body": [
    "The dashboard describes an energy-driven inflation squeeze being met by tighter money, not a reflation. Brent is back above $100, up 8% on the month and 43% over three months; the 10-year yield sits at 5.24%, up 44 basis points on the month and near the top of its 52-week range; and the dollar index is at a fresh 52-week high, up 2.5% on the month.",
    "The curve is the tell. Growth-led selloffs lift the long end fastest and carry copper with them. Here the 10-year has risen 44 basis points on the month against 34 for the 30-year, narrowing the 30y–10y gap by 11 basis points — a flattening led by the shorter maturity, the signature of markets pricing higher policy rates. Copper is flat on the month and down 3.4% this week; the 5.7% monthly rise in the copper/gold ratio is gold falling 5%, not copper rising. Gold sliding as yields and the dollar climb is a real-rate story, not fear.",
    "The dissent comes from bitcoin, up 7.7% on the month and buying 13% more gold than a month ago, which says liquidity and risk appetite have not drained. But it is flat on the week, and today's mix — Brent up 4.9%, copper down 1.2%, yields 4–6 basis points lower, dollar up 0.6% — reads as supply-priced oil weighing on growth. The bond market and dollar carry more weight: USD/CAD at a 52-week high with oil above $100 shows the dollar bid overriding even commodity exporters."
  ],
